{"type":"meta","version":"0.1.1","title":"The Water Cycle","created":"2026-08-16 04:49:00.266026+00:00","lastModified":"2026-08-16 04:49:00.266026+00:00"}
{"type":"extension","id":"semantic-anchors","data":[{"word":"Evaporation","description":"The process by which liquid water on Earth's surface is converted into water vapor and rises into the atmosphere due to heat energy from the sun.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Condensation","description":"The process by which water vapor cools and transforms back into liquid water droplets, forming clouds and fog in the atmosphere.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Precipitation","description":"Water that falls from the atmosphere to the Earth's surface in any form, such as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Transpiration","description":"The process by which plants absorb water through their roots and release water vapor through their leaves into the atmosphere.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Runoff","description":"Water from precipitation that flows over land surfaces and collects into streams, rivers, and eventually oceans rather than being absorbed into the ground.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Infiltration","description":"The process by which water seeps into the soil and rock layers beneath Earth's surface, replenishing groundwater reserves.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Water Vapor","description":"The gaseous state of water present in the atmosphere, formed through evaporation and transpiration, which drives the water cycle.","knowMoreUrl":null}]}
{"type":"layer","id":"7fa960a0-99dc-4214-8fd4-1eda055854b0","level":0,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"71a072ca-14b9-4689-86f1-2b6be7767a5d","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"The Water Cycle","summary":"The continuous movement of water through Earth's atmosphere, land, and oceans — driven by solar energy and gravity.","color":"#a6f5d8"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":null,"parentNodeId":null}
{"type":"layer","id":"b38de929-1ba1-4356-a130-243281c370dd","level":1,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"29c8640a-5574-40ea-ab7c-b58593c571d7","position":{"x":250,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Evaporation","summary":"Sun heats oceans, lakes & rivers — liquid water turns to vapour and rises into the atmosphere.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"00961190-08c3-4056-9e3a-2c1a88a8610e","position":{"x":77,"y":237},"data":{"label":"Transpiration","summary":"Plants release water vapour through tiny leaf pores (stomata), adding moisture to the air.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"d4ec1f9f-d584-4a2a-9e1a-8aa42b63a161","position":{"x":-202,"y":146},"data":{"label":"Condensation","summary":"Rising vapour cools at altitude and forms tiny water droplets — building clouds and fog.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"e716e250-8465-47cb-b66d-51fef4c8868a","position":{"x":-202,"y":-146},"data":{"label":"Precipitation","summary":"Droplets grow heavy and fall as rain, snow, sleet, or hail back to Earth's surface.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"58e77837-87c0-405e-be32-8ef4d6fc2ade","position":{"x":77,"y":-237},"data":{"label":"Collection","summary":"Water gathers in oceans, rivers, lakes & groundwater — ready to evaporate and restart the cycle.","color":"#a1c4fc"}}],"connections":[{"id":"f1c7d5a2-f2f1-40cb-aae5-331537394b2d","from":"29c8640a-5574-40ea-ab7c-b58593c571d7","to":"d4ec1f9f-d584-4a2a-9e1a-8aa42b63a161","data":{"label":"rises and cools into","style":"solid"}},{"id":"93460094-69e7-45d8-b059-7e901903300d","from":"00961190-08c3-4056-9e3a-2c1a88a8610e","to":"d4ec1f9f-d584-4a2a-9e1a-8aa42b63a161","data":{"label":"adds vapour to","style":"solid"}},{"id":"a7fdabe3-1efe-4bf0-bd48-2f42b638c72c","from":"d4ec1f9f-d584-4a2a-9e1a-8aa42b63a161","to":"e716e250-8465-47cb-b66d-51fef4c8868a","data":{"label":"leads to","style":"solid"}},{"id":"8f97cab8-2e7f-429b-8c5c-0438bd14c16e","from":"e716e250-8465-47cb-b66d-51fef4c8868a","to":"58e77837-87c0-405e-be32-8ef4d6fc2ade","data":{"label":"feeds into","style":"solid"}},{"id":"c579cb4a-dc2b-45f8-809a-2a72d65f9f81","from":"58e77837-87c0-405e-be32-8ef4d6fc2ade","to":"29c8640a-5574-40ea-ab7c-b58593c571d7","data":{"label":"restarts cycle via","style":"solid"}}],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"7fa960a0-99dc-4214-8fd4-1eda055854b0","parentNodeId":"71a072ca-14b9-4689-86f1-2b6be7767a5d"}
{"type":"layer","id":"675649c1-ad47-46b9-b811-2d981dda6517","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"630ce9a3-3463-49d9-b638-19d52802a11a","position":{"x":500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Sun's Energy","summary":"Solar radiation heats water surfaces, giving molecules enough energy to escape into the air as vapour.","color":"#fff699"}},{"id":"cb049a35-b0d8-4735-9a06-5baeaacc6d81","position":{"x":-500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Oceans & Lakes","summary":"Oceans supply ~90% of atmospheric water vapour; lakes and rivers contribute the rest.","color":"#a1c4fc"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"b38de929-1ba1-4356-a130-243281c370dd","parentNodeId":"29c8640a-5574-40ea-ab7c-b58593c571d7"}
{"type":"layer","id":"8b48fa80-8f60-413b-9df0-34986abc6048","level":2,"layout":"linear-vertical","nodes":[{"id":"ab741322-7374-48e7-8b2d-c6a848bdd21b","position":{"x":0,"y":-150},"data":{"label":"Rain","summary":"Liquid water droplets fall from clouds. Occurs when air temperatures stay above freezing.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"de5c608d-4719-40f7-917e-aabc779906bd","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Snow","summary":"Water vapour freezes into ice crystals, forming snowflakes. Needs below-freezing air temperatures.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"eac3a9ec-cfe9-4bce-969b-7869ca4ddcd6","position":{"x":0,"y":150},"data":{"label":"Hail","summary":"Balls of ice built up in thunderstorm clouds. Strong updrafts toss frozen droplets upward repeatedly before they fall.","color":"#faada5"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"b38de929-1ba1-4356-a130-243281c370dd","parentNodeId":"e716e250-8465-47cb-b66d-51fef4c8868a"}
{"type":"layer","id":"8bea1e2d-4a88-4cf4-90ce-20789566db7b","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"99119dca-04b4-4812-a845-593008037868","position":{"x":500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Cloud Formation","summary":"Clouds form when water vapor cools and condenses into millions of tiny droplets or ice crystals around dust particles high in the sky.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"4de0c34b-3a55-407f-9dea-11f9ac8d9535","position":{"x":-500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Dew & Frost","summary":"Dew forms when moist air cools near the ground and condenses on surfaces. Below freezing, that moisture turns to frost instead.","color":"#b7e7f3"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"b38de929-1ba1-4356-a130-243281c370dd","parentNodeId":"d4ec1f9f-d584-4a2a-9e1a-8aa42b63a161"}
{"type":"layer","id":"59c24548-2ceb-44b8-9b88-3cc19b669e71","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"dd97f3fe-ca6f-41a9-a1f9-947f4892b0c6","position":{"x":500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Oceans","summary":"Hold ~97% of all Earth's water. The largest reservoir in the water cycle, but water here is salty.","color":"#a1c4fc"}},{"id":"1a707b36-2192-4237-a04f-69fbc3634ae7","position":{"x":0,"y":500},"data":{"label":"Glaciers & Ice","summary":"Store ~2% of Earth's water as frozen freshwater. Ice caps and glaciers hold this water for thousands of years.","color":"#a1c4fc"}},{"id":"21e49a4b-7f4f-4163-97c7-e2c41471257e","position":{"x":-500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Groundwater","summary":"Rain seeps through soil into underground aquifers. A key source of fresh drinking water for humans.","color":"#a1c4fc"}},{"id":"24ac73e3-fae7-4483-bfd5-2070eb01baa0","position":{"x":0,"y":-500},"data":{"label":"Rivers & Lakes","summary":"Surface freshwater is a tiny fraction of total water. Essential for wildlife, farming, and daily human use.","color":"#a1c4fc"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[{"id":"9ab7873e-5d06-42b3-b367-0c4bfa9e8c09","nodes":["dd97f3fe-ca6f-41a9-a1f9-947f4892b0c6","1a707b36-2192-4237-a04f-69fbc3634ae7","21e49a4b-7f4f-4163-97c7-e2c41471257e","24ac73e3-fae7-4483-bfd5-2070eb01baa0"],"data":{"label":"Water Storage","formation":"natural","style":"dashed"}}],"parentLayerId":"b38de929-1ba1-4356-a130-243281c370dd","parentNodeId":"58e77837-87c0-405e-be32-8ef4d6fc2ade"}